s6-p2 : Block
s6-p2 = prose (cons "The language definitions of §5 are now available as inputs to further computation. Reflection is the point at which the language operates on its own descriptions: changing a grammar, an interpreter clause, or the context in which a received expression is interpreted changes what subsequent computations do. Reflection makes that activity directly programmable: the interpreter, its source language, its transformation, and the rationale connecting their behaviors can all be represented and operated on. Lisp reflection, definitional interpreters, normalization by evaluation, partial evaluation, and staged programming are foundational antecedents ([3](#ref-3), [5](#ref-5), [12](#ref-12), [13](#ref-13), [14](#ref-14), [15](#ref-15)). Here the relevant operations act on typed representations in the language itself." nil)
